    "result_text": "The suite has a single test class (`AcceptanceTest`), and it passes \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.\n\n## Summary\n\nI introduced address normalization for owner creation with minimal structural impact \u2014 **2 files touched**, and each changed function stays trivial.\n\n**New file** \u2014 `rest/advice/AddressNormalizer.java`: a tiny, cohesive helper (`WMC_other = 0`) with one straight-line method (`CC = 2`, only a null-guard) that trims, collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands `ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE` as whole words via `\\b` boundaries.\n\n**One-line change** \u2014 `OwnerFieldsValidationAdvice.validate()`: normalizes the address back onto the DTO right before the existing required-field check, mirroring how telephone is already normalized there. This single insertion delivers all three spec requirements at once:\n\n- **Store & return normalized** \u2014 the normalized value is written to the DTO, so it flows through the mapper into storage and the response (`\"  12  main  st \"` \u2192 `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`).\n- **Reject blank after normalization** \u2014 the subsequent `rejectIfBlank` now sees the normalized form (`\"   \"` \u2192 `\"\"` \u2192 400).\n- **Comparisons use the normalized form** \u2014 because the stored address is now canonical, the household-duplicate aspect (which runs after argument binding on the already-normalized DTO) and the mapper's shared household id both read the normalized value. No changes were needed to those files, which keeps files-touched \u2014 the biggest cost multiplier \u2014 at two.\n\nAll tests are green.",
